## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of various household chores (e.g., "wash the dishes," "sweep the floor," "make the bed," "water the plant"). Students identify the chores. The teacher asks, "What do you do at home?" and "Who helps your family?" to activate prior knowledge of the theme "Helping Around the House."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher takes a picture of "washing dishes" and explains, "This is 'wash the dishes'." (Teacher mimes washing). "It means to clean the plates and cups so they are tidy. Look!" (Points to the clean dishes in the picture). The teacher then takes a picture of "sweeping the floor" and says, "This is 'sweep the floor'." (Teacher mimes sweeping with a broom). "It means to clean the floor with a broom. We do it often." The teacher models clear pronunciation of /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I 'wash the dishes', do I clean the clothes or the plates?" (Plates!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I 'sweep the floor', do I use my hands or a broom?" (A broom!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping Around the House"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, simple, illustrated "Family Chore Chart" (digital or printed) for "The Daily Chores at Home." The chart lists chores (e.g., "wash the dishes," "feed the plant," "tidy up the bedroom") with clear pictures and a simple sentence for each, indicating who does it and how often (e.g., "Mom washes the dishes every day." "I feed the plant in the living room every day."). The chart includes chores like 'clean', 'cook', 'organize', 'dust', 'fold laundry', 'make the bed'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Discussing routines: Describing daily tasks and house chores according to context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is this chart about?" (Answer: Family chores / helping at home.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The chart says 'Dad cleans the kitchen every day'." (Students indicate True/False based on the chart content).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students point to the picture that matches a chore sentence read by the teacher (e.g., "Point to 'feed the plant'.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple ("I wash.", "He washes.", "I don't like to wash the dishes."), WH-questions ("What do you do at home?", "How often do you clean?"), Frequency adverbs ("every day," "often," "usually").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> nouns: dishes, house, chores, broom, floor, kitchen, family, task, table, plant, laundry, sheets, clothes, bathroom, bedroom, living room, porch; verbs: wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, organize, dust, fold, feed, mop, tidy up, throw, wipe, hang, change, make, water; adjectives: daily, tidy, organized, messy, important; adverbs: every day, often, usually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /w/ in "wash" and /s" in "sweep". Phonemic awareness: Recognition of closed and open syllables (e.g., closed syllable: "cook" and open syllable: "do").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking about chores: "What do you do at home?" Sharing experiences: "I help my mom."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Chore Match & Explain:</b> The teacher provides students with picture cards of chores (e.g., "wash the dishes," "sweep the floor," "make the bed") and separate word cards with simple descriptions (e.g., "You clean plates," "Use a broom," "Tidy your sleeping place"). Students work individually to match the picture to the description. In pairs, they then take turns showing a picture and saying the simple description, using gestures if it helps. E.g., Student A shows "sweep the floor" picture and says, "This is 'sweep the floor'. It means you clean the floor with a broom." Teacher monitors for accurate pronunciation of /w/ and /s/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Simplify the Instruction:</b> The teacher displays a few slightly more complex chore instructions (e.g., "Ensure all laundry is folded and placed in the appropriate drawers." or "Regularly remove dust from all furniture surfaces."). In small groups, students discuss and rephrase these into simpler A1.3-level sentences using present simple and target vocabulary. They use sentence frames like "This means you ___." or "It's about ___." or "You ___ the ___ every day/often." (E.g., "This means you fold clothes." "You dust the table usually.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Chore Explanation Challenge:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives 3-4 new chore words written on small slips of paper (e.g., "mop," "dust," "fold clothes," "water the plant"). Student A picks a word and has to explain it to Student B using only simple words, gestures (e.g., miming 'mopping' for 'mop'), or drawing a quick picture. Student B guesses the chore. They switch roles. They can ask, "What do you do at home?" to practice context.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping Around the House".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students present their "Chore Scheduling App" (a paper or digital schedule they created in previous lessons for Project 1) to a small group of peers. They act as "mediators," explaining the chores listed in their schedule to ensure their classmates understand.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> Students use their created chore schedules to describe daily routines and house chores, integrating the present simple tense and frequency adverbs, all within the context of "Helping Around the House."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining, collaborative interaction within the group, and successful communication/clarification of the chore schedule content.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Group Formation:</b> Students are organized into small groups of 3-4, ensuring mixed abilities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Project Presentation & Explanation:</b> Each student takes a turn presenting their "Chore Scheduling App." They point to each chore on their schedule and explain it to their group members using simple words, gestures (e.g., miming 'folding' for 'fold clothes'), and referring to any images or symbols on their schedule. Examples: "On Monday, I water the plant." "Every day, I make my bed." "My sister sweeps the floor often."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Questioning & Clarification:</b> Group members actively listen and ask simple WH-questions if they don't understand a chore (e.g., "What is 'dust'?" "How often do you clean the kitchen?"). The presenter must use mediation skills to rephrase, simplify, or use more gestures/visuals to ensure comprehension.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Teacher Monitoring & Support:</b> The teacher circulates among the groups, observing student interactions, listening for the correct use of present simple, frequency adverbs, chore vocabulary, and effective mediation strategies. The teacher provides minimal intervention, primarily encouraging student-to-student clarification.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy (observation checklist, rubric, or peer assessment).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and target language use. (An optional simple peer assessment checklist can also be used, where peers rate clarity of explanation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to explain their "Chore Scheduling App" to their peers, using simplified language and visual/gestural aids, will be assessed during the Performance stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explains chores using simple, A1.3-level language.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (chores, frequency adverbs) appropriately.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Employs visual aids (the 'app' itself) and/or gestures to clarify meaning.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to peer questions by simplifying or rephrasing explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces /w/ in "wash" and /s/ in "sweep" intelligibly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One chore I can explain easily to my friends is: _______. I can say: _________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I used a picture from my app or a gesture to help my friends understand when I explained: _______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student self-reflection and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when explaining your chore schedule to your friends today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using pictures or gestures help your friends understand the chores?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>What worked well:</b> The 'Chore Scheduling App' project was a strong motivator and a natural visual aid for mediation. Students were generally confident in presenting their work and many spontaneously used gestures to clarify words like 'sweep', 'fold', and 'water'. The structured practice in simplifying sentences helped build their mediation skills effectively.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson:</b> Some students still relied heavily on direct translation or struggled to find alternative simple words when rephrasing. For future mediation lessons, I could provide a 'simple words' bank or more sentence frames specifically for rephrasing and explaining. Additionally, dedicating time for students to practice *asking* clarifying questions could further enhance their interactive mediation skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Helping Around the House" theme and the 'Chore Scheduling App' project. The next lesson sequence will transition to a new theme and scenario, building on students' consolidated A1.3 grammar and vocabulary in a different real-world context, potentially focusing on daily routines related to school or free time activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>